  \begin{longtable}[]{@{}ccc@{}}
  \toprule\noalign{}
  X \textbackslash Y & 1 & 2 \\
  \midrule\noalign{}
  \endhead
  \bottomrule\noalign{}
  \endlastfoot
  0 & 0.27 & 0.15 \\
  1 & 0.05 & 0.18 \\
  2 & 0.29 & 0.06 \\
  \end{longtable}

  Qual a covariância entre as variáveis \(X\) e \(Y\)?

  \begin{enumerate}
  \def\labelenumii{(\alph{enumii})}
  \item
    \(-0.06\)
  \item
    \(0.37\)
  \item
    \(0.94\)
  \item
    \(1.23\)
  \item
    \(-0.05\)
  \end{enumerate}

  \textbf{Solution}\\
  Primeiramente devemos completar a tabela com as probabilidades
  marginais de \(X\) e \(Y\):

  \begin{longtable}[]{@{}cccc@{}}
  \toprule\noalign{}
  X Y & 1 & 2 & P(X=x) \\
  \midrule\noalign{}
  \endhead
  \bottomrule\noalign{}
  \endlastfoot
  0 & 0.27 & 0.15 & 0.42 \\
  1 & 0.05 & 0.18 & 0.23 \\
  2 & 0.29 & 0.06 & 0.35 \\
  P(Y=y) & 0.61 & 0.39 & 1 \\
  \end{longtable}

  Em seguida, calculamos

  \[\begin{aligned}
  E(X) &=& 0\times0.42+1\times0.23+2\times0.35 = 0.93\\
  E(Y) &=& 1\times0.61+2\times0.39 = 1.39
  \end{aligned}\]

  A distribuição do produto é dada por

  \begin{longtable}[]{@{}ccccc@{}}
  \toprule\noalign{}
  k & 0 & 1 & 2 & 4 \\
  \midrule\noalign{}
  \endhead
  \bottomrule\noalign{}
  \endlastfoot
  P(XY = k) & 0.42 & 0.05 & 0.47 & 0.06 \\
  \end{longtable}

  De modo que
  \[E(XY) = 0\times0.42+1\times0.05+2\times0.47+4\times0.06 = 1.23\] e,
  portanto, \[\text{Cov}(X,Y) = E(XY)-E(X)E(Y) = -0.06.\]

